From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angel Alvarez Pascua <angel.alvarez.pascua@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [SQL] Stop rendering plan trees once maxPlanStringLength is reached

### What changes were proposed in this pull request?

Make `spark.sql.maxPlanStringLength` stop plan-tree rendering once the configured
limit has been filled, rather than continuing to render every remaining node and
discarding the resulting text.

`PlanStringConcat` gains a scoped `withEarlyStop` helper. Bounded rendering is
kept local to individual tree/detail rendering operations (`TreeNode.treeString`,
`QueryPlan.appendBounded`, and formatted explain rendering). It is deliberately
not wrapped around an entire `QueryExecution.explainString` or
`ExplainUtils.processPlan` call: analysis, optimization, physical planning,
statistics, and other explain work must keep their existing execution semantics
even when the output limit is zero.

Because an abandoned tree traversal no longer knows the full untruncated string
length, early-stopped output uses `... more characters` (or `Truncated plan of
unknown length` at a zero limit). Direct `PlanStringConcat.append` callers still
run to completion and keep the exact character-count message.

### Why are the changes needed?

`StringConcat.append` already stops retaining text after
`spark.sql.maxPlanStringLength`, but `TreeNode.generateTreeString` continues
walking the tree and materializing every node string. On very large plans this
means the configuration bounds retained string memory without bounding the CPU
spent producing text that is immediately discarded.

SPARK-59023 lets users opt out of SQL UI plan descriptions entirely with
`spark.sql.ui.explainMode=none`. This change is complementary for callers that
still request bounded plan text. It targets plan-tree string rendering only; it
does not claim to bound unrelated work such as cost-statistics computation,
operator-id assignment, codegen discovery, or `SparkPlanInfo` construction.

### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?

Only for plan strings that are already truncated: when the tree walk is stopped
early, Spark can no longer report the exact number of characters that were not
rendered.

### How was this patch tested?

* Added `PlanStringConcat` tests for early stop and a zero-length limit.
* Added a `TreeNodeSuite` test proving that nodes after the limit are not
  rendered.
* Added `QueryExecutionSuite` coverage proving that a zero output limit does not
  skip physical planning for simple or extended explain paths.
* Existing SPARK-31916 direct-append tests continue to require the exact
  truncation count.

### Was this patch authored or co-authored using generative AI tooling?

